\paragraph{Degeneration under confirmation bias.}
In practice, self-extracted constraints are tightly coupled to their source answer: $c_m$ derived from trace $t_i$ typically entails $a_i$ ($\sigma(c_m, a_i) \approx 1$). While ${\sim}$92\% of constraints are technically discriminative across candidates (\S\ref{sec:extraction-taxonomy}), their discriminative direction mirrors the model's own answer distribution rather than providing independent signal. Constraints from majority-answer traces form a mutually consistent set; because these constraints use trace-specific variable names (56.4\% of cross-answer pairs share zero Z3 variables), MAX-SAT treats them as logically independent and retains nearly all of them ($|\mathcal{C}^{\setminus}| \approx 0$). The penalty term vanishes, and the score function degenerates:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:degenerate}
\text{score}(a) \approx \sum_{\substack{c \in \mathcal{C}^* \\ \text{source}(c) \to a}} w_c,
\end{equation}
which reduces to a weighted count of traces supporting $a$, equivalent to majority voting when weights are uniform. Removing the ${\sim}$8\% non-discriminative constraints yields zero accuracy improvement (\S\ref{sec:extraction-taxonomy}), confirming that the problem is not a lack of discrimination but confirmation bias: discriminative constraints reinforce the majority answer that SC would already select. The ablation in \S\ref{sec:ablation} confirms this empirically: MAX-SAT, count-only, and random-weight baselines produce nearly identical accuracy.

